.TH B38 1 "November 2026" "libb38 0.3.1" "libb38"
.SH NAME
b38_add, b38_sub, b38_mul, b38_div_rem, b38_neg, b38_to_i128, b38_from_i128, b38_cmp, b38_ratio_add, b38_ratio_sub, b38_ratio_mul, b38_ratio_div, b38_ratio_neg, b38_ratio_cmp, b38_ratio_to_radix, b38_ratio_from_radix, b38_free, b38_version \-
balanced base-38 exact arithmetic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <b38.h>
.sp
.BI "char *b38_add(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_sub(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_mul(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_neg(const char *" a ");"
.BI "int b38_div_rem(const char *" a ", const char *" b ", char **" out_q ", char **" out_r ");"
.BI "int b38_to_i128(const char *" a ", __int128 *" out ");"
.BI "int b38_from_i128(__int128 " v ", char **" out ");"
.BI "int b38_cmp(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_add(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_sub(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_mul(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_div(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_neg(const char *" a ");"
.BI "int b38_ratio_cmp(const char *" a ", const char *" b ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_to_radix(const char *" a ");"
.BI "char *b38_ratio_from_radix(const char *" a ");"
.BI "void b38_free(char *" s ");"
.BI "unsigned int b38_version(void);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libb38
is a drop-in exact-arithmetic layer: it computes with balanced base-38
strings exactly, with no rounding, no overflow surprises, and no
binary-to-decimal conversion tax. It wraps the balanced base-38 lane;
the arithmetic laws and the signed-i128 refusal contract are proved in
Lean for the mathematical model, and the Rust implementation is tested
against them.
.PP
The two string operators,
.BR b38_add ()
and
.BR b38_sub (),
add or subtract (subtraction IS addition of the negation, the
reflection law) two balanced base-38 strings and return a newly
allocated string holding the exact result.
.BR b38_mul ()
returns the exact product.
.BR b38_neg ()
returns the negation.
.PP
.BR b38_div_rem ()
computes the Euclidean quotient and remainder of
.I a
/ 
.IR b :
the remainder always lies in \fB0..|b|\fR. The quotient string is
written to
.I *out_q
and the remainder string to
.IR *out_r ;
returns 0 on success, \-1 on refusal.
.PP
.BR b38_to_i128 ()
parses a string and writes its exact value to
.IR *out ,
the honest shore door: a string whose value sits beyond the i128 shore
refuses (\-1), never wraps. Returns 0 on success.
.PP
.BR b38_from_i128 ()
formats an exact \fB__int128\fR value as its canonical balanced base-38
string, written to
.I *out
(the caller frees it). The value never refuses; only allocation
failure returns \-1.
.PP
.BR b38_cmp ()
orders two strings as exact integers, like
.BR strcmp (3):
negative if
.I a
<
.IR b ,
zero if equal, positive if
.I a
>
.IR b .
It returns \-1, 0, or +1; a bad input returns
.B B38_CMP_REFUSAL
(1000000), a value no valid comparison ever returns.
.PP
The v3 ration doors are THE PROMISE'S STORE: exact fractions, never
views. The string
.RS
.I p/q
.RE
means the reduced fraction with the sign on the numerator and a
positive denominator (\fB"+5/+4"\fR); a bare integer rides as
.IR n/1 ,
zero is
.BR "0" .
.BR b38_ratio_add (),
.BR b38_ratio_sub (),
.BR b38_ratio_mul (),
and
.BR b38_ratio_div ()
add, subtract, multiply, and divide (by the reciprocal, refusing a
zero divisor) exactly.
.BR b38_ratio_neg ()
negates.
.BR b38_ratio_cmp ()
orders two fractions exactly by cross-products, returning \-1, 0, +1
and
.B B38_CMP_REFUSAL
when a cross-product crosses the i128 shore.
.PP
The radix doors are VIEWS over the fraction store, never the storage.
.BR b38_ratio_to_radix ()
hands back the exact base-38 expansion with its recurring block wrapped
in trailing parentheses: a third is
.BR "0.(cp)" ,
three quarters is
.BR "0.sj" ,
a thirty-eighth is
.BR "0.1" .
The Euclidean (floor) split puts the sign on the integer part, so minus
one half reads
.BR "\-1.j" .
A period at or beyond 2^16 digits, or a long-division step whose 38*r
product crosses the i128 shore, refuses (NULL). 
.BR b38_ratio_from_radix ()
re-reads such a string into the canonical fraction
(\fB"0.(cp)"\fR back to
.BR "+1/+3" ),
refusing malformed strings and any view whose closed form crosses the
shore \-\- a long view refuses to re-read rather than lie.
.PP
The caller owns every returned string and must free it with
.BR b38_free ().
All
.B libb38
functions take a non-NULL string arguments (NULL is undefined
behaviour); they refuse bad input loudly rather than guess.
.PP
.BR b38_version ()
returns the C-ABI version (currently 3). A caller should check it
before trusting the surface; it is bumped on every breaking change.
.SH THE STRING FORMAT
A balanced base-38 number is a signed, most-significant-first string
over the 38-character alphabet
.BR "0-9 a-z _ ~" ,
where the 38 symbols stand for the digit values 0 through 37.
.PP
The sign is an optional leading
.B +
or
.BR \- .
The string
.B 0
is zero (the Void). There are no leading zeros, and every integer value
has exactly one string. Examples:
.TS
tab(@);
l l .
.+f@plus fifteen
.-7@minus seven
.1z@1\*38+35, i.e. seventy-three
.1_@seventy-four (a carry lands on the highest gap digit)
.+6c@two hundred forty
.TE
.SH RETURN VALUE
The string operators return NULL on any error: malformed input (empty,
leading zero, or a character outside the alphabet), a result whose
magnitude exceeds the i128 shore, or allocation failure. The integer
doors refuse with \-1 (and
.BR b38_cmp ,
with
.BR B38_CMP_REFUSAL ).
Nothing ever wraps; every refusal is loud.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <b38.h>

int main(void) {
    char *s = b38_mul("f", "g");  /* 15 * 16 = 240 -> "+6c" */
    if (s == NULL) { fprintf(stderr, "refused\\n"); return 1; }
    puts(s);
    b38_free(s);
    __int128 v = 0;
    if (b38_to_i128("+528", &v) != 0) { fprintf(stderr, "refused\\n"); return 1; }
    printf("%lld\\n", (long long)v);  /* 7304 */
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH BUILDING
.RS
From a fresh clone of this workspace, the proof is three lines,
in this order (the -I/-L paths are relative to the WORKSPACE ROOT, where
cargo build lands target/debug/liblibb38.so):
.PP
cargo build -p libb38
.PP
cc crates/libb38/examples/smoke.c -I crates/libb38/include -L target/debug -Wl,-rpath,$PWD/target/debug -llibb38 -o smoke
.PP
./smoke
.PP
libb38 version 3 (or newer) / ALL SMOKE CHECKS PASS
.RE
